// ================= ABSTRACT =================
push(H1("Abstract"));
push(body("Quantitative structure–activity relationship (QSAR) modelling becomes unreliable when the number of molecular descriptors (p) approaches or exceeds the number of compounds (n) — the small-n, large-p problem that is characteristic of antimicrobial and natural-product datasets. Two descriptor-selection routes dominate the literature: computing very large descriptor pools and reducing them by correlation and univariate statistics, or pre-selecting a small set of chemically interpretable descriptors. Using 31 monoterpenoids tested against five pneumonia-associated bacterial strains, we show that the first route, when a supervised univariate filter (effect-size / Wilcoxon selection) is applied to the whole dataset before cross-validation, produces optimistically biased performance: the ordinary permutation test keeps the selected descriptors fixed and is therefore circular. Under fully nested validation — repeating descriptor selection inside every cross-validation fold and permuting the entire pipeline — the apparent predictive ability collapses (e.g. Haemophilus influenzae cross-validated Q² 0.43 → −0.14, not significant; Streptococcus pyogenes 0.52 → −0.11, not significant). Importantly, a weak but genuinely significant signal survived nesting for Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pneumoniae (Q² 0.16 and 0.19; p = 0.013 and 0.010), so the procedure is not worthless — it can be meaningful for some endpoints provided it is honestly validated. The second route — 25 literature-derived, interpretable descriptors reduced only by unsupervised correlation grouping (which uses no activity labels and is therefore leakage-free) — recovered an honestly significant, moderate signal for H. influenzae, S. aureus and S. pneumoniae (Q² 0.29–0.39, p ≤ 0.003) even with a single latent variable. A two-stage variable-importance (VIP) selection, in which high-VIP descriptors from a first model are re-used in a second, reproduces the same optimistic bias unless the VIP step is itself nested. We conclude with practical guidance for descriptor selection under an unfavourable n:p ratio."));
push(new Paragraph({ spacing:{after:60}, children:[new TextRun({text:"Scientific contribution", bold:true, font:FONT, size:22})]}));
push(body("We show, on a real small-sample antimicrobial dataset, that the apparent success of supervised descriptor-selection filters in small-n QSAR is largely an artefact of selection bias, and that only fully nested validation with whole-pipeline permutation gives an honest performance estimate. We further demonstrate that an unsupervised, correlation-only reduction of a small, theory-driven descriptor set is leakage-free and recovers a genuine signal where the data-driven route fails, and we provide a decision framework for choosing and validating descriptors when p ≈ or > n."));
push(new Paragraph({ spacing:{after:120}, children:[
  new TextRun({text:"Keywords: ", bold:true, font:FONT, size:22}),
  new TextRun({text:"molecular descriptors; QSAR; feature selection; small sample size; n:p ratio; nested cross-validation; selection bias; permutation testing; PLS-DA; monoterpenoids", font:FONT, size:22})]}));

// ================= INTRODUCTION =================
push(H1("Introduction"));
push(body("Quantitative structure–activity relationship (QSAR) modelling predicts biological activity from molecular structure and is an established tool in early drug discovery and natural-product research [refs]. Its central premise is that activity is encoded in quantifiable molecular features whose relationship to a measured endpoint can be captured by a statistical model. In practice, why some closely related compounds are active while others are not can rarely be explained by a handful of classical physicochemical parameters; capturing the relevant structural and electronic information usually requires large, computationally generated descriptor sets. This is the origin of the statistical difficulty addressed here."));
push(body("Modern platforms generate thousands of descriptors from structure alone, whereas activity-driven studies — natural-product discovery and early medicinal chemistry in particular — are limited to tens of compounds by synthesis, isolation and assay costs [refs]. The number of descriptors (p) then greatly exceeds the number of samples (n): the small-n, large-p problem, or curse of dimensionality. In this regime the multivariate methods most used in QSAR can produce apparent separation of random data; Ruiz-Pérez and colleagues showed with synthetic data that partial least squares discriminant analysis (PLS-DA) is reliable only around a sample-to-feature ratio of roughly 2:1 [Ruiz-Pérez et al.]. Controlling the n:p ratio is therefore a prerequisite for any trustworthy small-sample model, not a cosmetic refinement."));
push(body("Two descriptor-selection strategies recur in the literature. The first computes a very large descriptor pool from one or more libraries and reduces it — typically by correlation filtering followed by a univariate statistical criterion (for example a rank-sum test with an effect-size threshold), sometimes followed by re-selection of high variable-importance-in-projection (VIP) descriptors. The second pre-selects a small number of chemically interpretable descriptors on mechanistic grounds and reduces residual redundancy by correlation only. Both are widely used, but their statistical validity in the small-n regime is rarely examined critically."));
push(body("A crucial and under-appreciated distinction separates the two. Correlation filtering between descriptors is unsupervised — it never uses the activity labels — and is therefore leakage-free. A univariate effect-size filter, or a VIP-based re-selection, is supervised: it uses the labels. When such a supervised selection is performed on the whole dataset before cross-validation, the left-out samples have already influenced which descriptors are retained, so the cross-validated error and any permutation test that keeps the selection fixed are optimistically biased [Ambroise & McLachlan; Varma & Simon; Krstajić et al.]. With p ≫ n this bias is severe, because some descriptors always separate a given small dataset by chance [Golbraikh & Tropsha; Rücker et al.]. An honest estimate requires that the entire supervised selection be repeated inside every cross-validation fold (nested validation) and that permutation tests randomise the labels through the complete pipeline."));
push(body("Here we examine both selection routes on a previously published dataset of 31 monoterpenoids tested against five pneumonia-associated bacterial strains [Werle et al. 2026] — a representative small-sample scenario. We first show that the large-pool route with a supervised effect-size filter yields high apparent performance that is largely removed by nested validation, but that a weak, genuinely significant signal nonetheless survives for two strains. We then show that the small, interpretable, unsupervised-reduced descriptor set is leakage-free and recovers a genuine moderate signal for three strains. Finally, we quantify the bias of the two-stage VIP procedure and give practical guidance for descriptor selection under an unfavourable n:p ratio."));
push(authorNote("Introduction: [ref]/[Name]-Platzhalter in nummerierte Referenzen umwandeln. Die method­ischen Kernreferenzen (Ambroise & McLachlan 2002; Varma & Simon 2006; Krstajić et al. 2014; Golbraikh & Tropsha 2002; Rücker et al. 2007) sind in der Referenzliste bereits verifiziert enthalten."));

// ================= MATERIALS AND METHODS =================
push(H1("Materials and methods"));
push(H2("Data set"));
push(body("The workflow was demonstrated on a previously published data set of 31 monoterpenoids tested against five pneumonia-associated bacterial strains (Haemophilus influenzae ATCC 49247, Pseudomonas aeruginosa ATCC 27853, Staphylococcus aureus ATCC 29213, Streptococcus pneumoniae ATCC 49619 and Streptococcus pyogenes ATCC 19615) [Werle et al. 2026]. Antibacterial activity in the liquid (broth) phase of the broth microdilution volatilization assay was the endpoint; for each strain compounds were assigned a binary class (active vs. non-active) from their mean minimum inhibitory concentration, giving five independent classification endpoints. The active/non-active split was 10/21 (H. influenzae), 3/28 (P. aeruginosa), 5/26 (S. aureus), 6/25 (S. pneumoniae) and 11/20 (S. pyogenes). P. aeruginosa, with only three actives, was treated as descriptive only and excluded from predictive claims."));
push(H2("The data set and its limitations"));
push(body("This data set was chosen deliberately for its imperfections, not despite them. With only 31 compounds it is small, and the active/inactive split is imbalanced and endpoint-specific — from a relatively balanced 10/21 and 11/20 (H. influenzae, S. pyogenes) to 5/26 and 6/25 (S. aureus, S. pneumoniae) and an extreme 3/28 for P. aeruginosa, which has too few actives for predictive modelling and is reported as descriptive only. The monoterpenoids form a narrow, structurally homogeneous chemical class, which supports mechanistic interpretation within the class but limits descriptor variance and external applicability. Activity was obtained by dichotomising minimum inhibitory concentrations from the standardised broth microdilution volatilisation assay against defined ATCC reference strains; the assay is reproducible, but dichotomisation discards information and makes the labels threshold-dependent, and borderline compounds can influence the small active class disproportionately. No external test set is available, so all estimates are internal. Against 2,208 cleaned descriptors, the initial sample-to-feature ratio of roughly 1:71 is far from the ~2:1 regarded as adequate for PLS-DA."));
push(body("These properties are exactly why the data set is instructive here. The aim of this study is to expose the problems and difficulties of descriptor selection followed by PLS-DA in the small-n, large-p regime; a small, imbalanced and chemically narrow set makes selection bias, overfitting and the instability of supervised selection visible in a way that a large, balanced, chemically diverse \u201cideal\u201d data set would not. A near-perfect data set would have produced cleaner models but would have been far less suited to the critical, methodological discussion that is the purpose of this work. Accordingly, all results are framed as associations within this data set rather than as validated predictors of new compounds."));

push(H2("Route A — large multi-library descriptor pool"));
push(H3("Stage 1 — Descriptor generation"));
push(body("Canonical SMILES from PubChem [Kim et al.] were used to compute descriptors on the ChemDes platform [Dong et al.] from five libraries — Chemopy (1,135 descriptors) [Cao et al.], PaDEL (1,975 descriptors) [Yap], RDKit (196 descriptors), BlueDesc (174 descriptors) and Pybel (27 descriptors) — giving 3,507 descriptors in total. Non-numerical, missing-value and autocorrelation descriptors were removed, together with exact duplicates and constant/near-zero-variance descriptors, reducing this initial pool of 3,507 to 2,208 numeric descriptors. No descriptor-class partitioning was applied and no OECD QSAR Toolbox descriptors were used."));
push(H3("Stage 2 — Correlation-based feature grouping (unsupervised)"));
push(body("Across the retained descriptors, Spearman (ρ) and Pearson (r) coefficients were computed and a descriptor pair treated as redundant when |ρ| > 0.7 or |r| > 0.7. Redundant descriptors were consolidated into feature groups by an iterative most-connected-first procedure adapted from Comesaña et al.; uncorrelated descriptors were retained individually. This step uses no activity labels and is therefore leakage-free."));
push(H3("Stage 3 — Supervised effect-size filtering"));
push(body("Each descriptor surviving Stage 2 was tested for its ability to discriminate active from non-active compounds with the Wilcoxon rank-sum test (Mann–Whitney U). The effect size was taken as the rank-biserial correlation (equivalently r = Z/√N) and interpreted by Cohen's guidelines; descriptors with p ≤ 0.05 and |r| > 0.3 were retained, and within each feature group the highest-effect-size descriptor was kept as representative. The p-values were not adjusted for multiple testing (no Benjamini–Hochberg or comparable correction); across the ~2,208 tests this inflates the false-positive rate, which is one reason the resulting selection must be validated by nesting rather than reported directly. This step is supervised."));
push(H3("Stage 4 — Final selection and n:p constraint"));
push(body("Representatives and singletons were pooled and, where a fixed model size was required, the highest-effect-size descriptors were retained to keep the sample-to-feature ratio near 2:1 (p ≤ n/2 ≈ 15 for n = 31) [Ruiz-Pérez et al.]."));

push(H2("Route B — small, literature-derived interpretable descriptors"));
push(body("Independently, 25 interpretable two-dimensional descriptors were selected a priori from the monoterpenoid structure–activity literature, spanning constitution (molecular weight, carbon and oxygen counts, heavy-atom and ring counts), saturation/shape (fraction of sp3 carbon, aliphatic/aromatic rings, C=C count, stereocentres, degree of unsaturation), functional groups (hydrogen-bond donors/acceptors and SMARTS indicators for aldehyde, ketone, carboxylic acid, phenol, ether, ester/lactone and aliphatic alcohol) and physicochemistry (Crippen logP and molar refractivity, Ertl TPSA, Labute ASA). All descriptors were computed with RDKit from neutral, canonical SMILES, without 3D optimisation or pH/protonation correction; rotatable bonds used the strict definition, aromaticity the RDKit default model, and stereocentres included unspecified centres. Exact definitions and SMARTS are given in the Additional file. Because this set is fixed a priori from external knowledge, it uses no activity information (leakage-free by construction). Residual redundancy was removed by the same unsupervised correlation grouping as in Stage 2 (|ρ| or |r| > 0.7), with rare binary indicators (fewer than four positive compounds) kept as singletons because their correlations are unstable at n = 31; this reduced the 25 descriptors to 13."));

push(H2("PLS-DA modelling and validation"));
push(body("Descriptor sets were modelled by PLS-DA with autoscaling and leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV). The number of latent variables was reported both at a parsimonious nLV = 1 and at the Q²-optimal value; nLV = 1 is the conservative, selection-independent estimate. Model quality was reported as the apparent explained variance (R²Y), the cross-validated Q² and the area under the ROC curve (AUC), with balanced accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and the Matthews correlation coefficient for classification. Statistical significance was assessed by a permutation test with 2,000 random relabelings, p = (1 + #{permuted ≥ observed}) / (1 + n)."));
push(body("The type of permutation test was matched to the selection step. For the supervised Route A, the effect-size filter and feature grouping were repeated inside every LOOCV fold on the training data only, and the 2,000 permutations randomised the labels through the complete pipeline (fully nested validation with whole-pipeline y-scrambling). For the unsupervised Route B, a simple permutation with the fixed descriptor set is already honest, because no supervised selection precedes the model; apparent and cross-validated performance therefore coincide. The two-stage VIP procedure was evaluated in a naive variant (VIP > 1 descriptors chosen on the whole dataset, then re-modelled) and a nested variant (VIP selection repeated inside each fold) to quantify its bias. Multiplicity across the five endpoints was addressed by a Bonferroni threshold (α = 0.01)."));
push(body("Concretely, each permutation randomised only the class-label vector; the descriptor matrix was left unchanged. For every permuted labelling the entire Route A pipeline was recomputed from scratch — the rank-biserial effect size and its p-value for all 2,208 descriptors, the p ≤ 0.05 and |r| > 0.3 filter, the correlation grouping, the top-k ranking and the PLS-DA fit — inside each leave-one-out fold on the training partition only. No effect size was reassigned between descriptors, and nothing selected on the real labels was carried over; because the descriptor ranks are label-independent they were pre-computed once, whereas the label-dependent effect size was re-evaluated in every permutation. This ensures that the advantage of choosing the best-separating descriptors from a large pool is itself contained in the null distribution — which an ordinary permutation that holds the selected descriptors fixed does not achieve."));
push(H2("Software"));
push(body("Route A descriptor calculation used the ChemDes platform (Chemopy, PaDEL, RDKit, BlueDesc and Pybel libraries); the correlation analysis and the illustrative PLS-DA score plot used MetaboAnalyst 5.0. Route B descriptors, the correlation-based reduction, all cross-validated and nested PLS-DA models, the permutation tests and VIP calculations were implemented in Python (RDKit 2026.03.3, scikit-learn, SciPy, pandas). All analysis scripts and data tables are provided as Additional files to ensure full reproducibility."));
push(H3("Use of large language models"));
push(body("A large language model (Claude Opus 4.8; Anthropic, PBC) was used, under the authors' direction and supervision, to assist with implementing the statistical-analysis code (in particular the nested cross-validation and whole-pipeline permutation procedures), generating figures, and drafting and editing the manuscript text. It was not used to generate or interpret the underlying biological activity data. All code, analyses, results and interpretations were reviewed and verified by the authors, who take full responsibility for the content. In accordance with COPE and Springer Nature policy, the model does not meet the criteria for authorship and is not listed as an author."));
push(authorNote("Software-Referenzen (ChemDes, PaDEL, Chemopy, RDKit, BlueDesc, Pybel, MetaboAnalyst, scikit-learn, SciPy) mit Version als nummerierte Zitate ergänzen. LLM-Offenlegung oben ist ein Vorschlag nach Springer-Nature/COPE-Vorgabe – bei Bedarf anpassen."));

// ================= CASE STUDY / RESULTS =================
push(H1("Case study: antimicrobial monoterpenoids"));
push(body("Both routes were applied to the 31-monoterpenoid data set. Route A started from 3,507 descriptors (five ChemDes libraries), reduced to 2,208 after cleaning — an initial ratio of about 1:71 for 31 compounds — which the selection stages reduced to strain-specific representative sets (31–53 descriptors; Table 1). Route B used the 25 pre-selected descriptors reduced to 13 by unsupervised correlation grouping. The framework is summarised in Fig. 1."));
push(img("/home/claude/ms_unpack/word/media/image1.jpg", 560, 249, "jpg"));
push(legend("1","Overview of the two descriptor-selection routes and the validation strategy. Route A (large multi-library pool → correlation grouping → supervised effect-size filter → n:p-constrained selection) is validated by fully nested cross-validation; Route B (small, literature-derived interpretable set → unsupervised correlation grouping) is leakage-free and validated by a simple permutation test."));
push(authorNote("Fig. 1 ist noch die ursprüngliche Vierstufen-Grafik. Für die überarbeitete Aussage bitte um die zweite (unsupervised) Route und den Nested-Validation-Schritt ergänzen, oder als Schema neu zeichnen."));

push(H2("Route A: apparent performance is removed by nested validation"));
push(body("With the supervised effect-size filter applied before cross-validation, Route A produced high apparent performance across strains (single-component models, apparent Q² up to ~0.5–0.9 and permutation p ≈ 0.0005). These values, however, are optimistically biased: the permutation test kept the label-selected descriptors fixed and therefore tested only the modelling step, not the selection. When the effect-size filter and feature grouping were repeated inside every fold and the whole pipeline was permuted, the cross-validated ability fell sharply (Table 1, Fig. 2, Fig. 3). For H. influenzae Q² fell from 0.43 to −0.14 (p = 0.15) and for S. pyogenes from 0.52 to −0.11 (p = 0.13) — neither better than the class-mean predictor and neither significant. For S. aureus and S. pneumoniae a weak but significant signal remained (nested Q² 0.16 and 0.19; p = 0.013 and 0.010). P. aeruginosa retained perfect but uninterpretable values (three actives) and is reported descriptively only."));

