push(tblCap("Table 1|  Cross-validated predictive ability (Q²) of the three analyses and the number of descriptors entering PLS-DA. Descriptors (A / B): strain-specific representative set for Route A (correlation grouping + effect-size selection) and the fixed 13-descriptor set for Route B. Route A apparent = supervised selection before cross-validation (optimistic). Route A nested = selection repeated inside every fold with whole-pipeline permutation. Route B = literature-derived descriptors, unsupervised reduction, one latent variable. Permutation p in parentheses; * significant at p < 0.05; n.s. not significant. † P. aeruginosa has only three actives and is descriptive only. All values are from the reproducible Python pipeline (Additional files)."));

push(img("/mnt/user-data/outputs/Fig_three_way_comparison.png", 520, 436, "png"));
push(legend("2","Three-way comparison of PLS-DA validation across the five strains. Grouped bars show cross-validated Q² (top) and AUC (bottom) for Route A apparent (grey), Route A nested (orange) and Route B literature-derived, nLV = 1 (green). Asterisks mark models significant by permutation (p < 0.05); P. aeruginosa (hatched) is an artefact with only three actives. Apparent Route A performance is largely removed by nested validation, whereas the leakage-free Route B recovers a genuine signal for three strains."));

push(img("/mnt/user-data/outputs/Fig_nested_permutation_all5.png", 470, 666, "png"));
push(legend("3","Fully nested permutation test of Route A for all five strains (2,000 permutations of the entire pipeline). Histograms show the null distribution of Q² (left) and AUC (right); the observed value (solid line) is compared with the 95th percentile of the null (dashed). The observed values fall inside the null distribution for H. influenzae and S. pyogenes (not significant) and exceed it only weakly for S. aureus and S. pneumoniae."));

push(H2("Route B: a leakage-free interpretable set recovers a genuine signal"));
push(body("The 13 literature-derived descriptors, reduced only by unsupervised correlation, gave an honestly significant, moderate signal for H. influenzae, S. aureus and S. pneumoniae even with a single latent variable (Q² = 0.29, 0.39 and 0.35; AUC 0.82–0.85; p = 0.001, 0.003 and 0.003; Table 1, Fig. 2). Because the reduction uses no labels, the simple permutation test is already honest and apparent and cross-validated performance coincide. These are precisely the strains that Route A lost or weakened under nesting. S. pyogenes remained weak and non-significant (Q² = 0.06) despite having the most actives, indicating an endpoint-specific rather than a sample-size-driven effect. Under the Bonferroni threshold (α = 0.01) H. influenzae and S. pneumoniae remained significant and S. aureus was borderline (p(AUC) = 0.018)."));

push(H2("The two-stage VIP procedure reproduces the bias"));
push(body("A procedure sometimes used in the literature — fitting a first PLS-DA, keeping only high-VIP descriptors and re-fitting — was evaluated. When the VIP selection was performed on the whole dataset (naive), the subsequent Q² was consistently higher than when the VIP step was repeated inside each fold (nested); for example H. influenzae 0.29 → 0.16 and S. pneumoniae 0.53 → 0.31. The difference is the optimistic bias introduced by the label-dependent VIP selection, exactly the leakage seen for the effect-size filter. The descriptors reaching VIP > 1 for each strain are listed in the Additional file."));

// ---- top-15 subsection ----
push(H2("Does the n:p-constrained (top-15) selection rescue Route A?"));
push(body("The original design of Route A retained the 15 highest-effect-size descriptors per strain to keep the sample-to-feature ratio near 2:1. We evaluated this top-15 model with the same honest machinery (Table 1B, Fig. 2b, Fig. 3b). Constraining to 15 descriptors did not restore the apparent performance under nesting: for H. influenzae the nested Q² remained negative (−0.16, p = 0.18) and for S. aureus it fell to −0.04 (p = 0.06). More tellingly, the significance pattern shifted relative to the full representative sets — S. pyogenes now became significant (nested Q² = 0.17, p = 0.015) while S. aureus lost significance — although the underlying data were identical. This instability, in which the set of apparently significant endpoints depends on the exact size of the supervised selection, is itself a symptom of the small-n regime: keeping the n:p ratio near 2:1 is necessary but not sufficient, because the supervised selection remains label-dependent and must still be validated by nesting. S. pneumoniae remained significant under both selections, and P. aeruginosa remained an artefact."));
