push(img("/mnt/user-data/outputs/Fig_nested_permutation_top15.png", 470, 666, "png"));
push(legend("3b","Fully nested permutation test of the top-15 Route A models for all five strains (2,000 permutations of the entire pipeline). Layout as in Fig. 3. Observed values are significant only for S. pneumoniae and S. pyogenes (P. aeruginosa is an artefact); H. influenzae and S. aureus remain within the null distribution."));

// ---- selection-criterion comparison (Table 1C) ----
push(H2("Effect-size versus VIP selection: the significant strains change with the criterion"));
push(body("The 15 descriptors can be chosen either by effect size or by variable importance in projection (VIP). We compared both criteria on identical data with identical apparent and fully nested validation (2,000 whole-pipeline permutations each; Table 1C, Fig. 2c). Apparent performance was comparable, but the honest (nested) verdict differed and, most importantly, the set of significant strains changed with the criterion. Under effect-size selection, S. pneumoniae and S. pyogenes were significant; under VIP selection, S. aureus and S. pyogenes were significant. S. aureus and S. pneumoniae therefore exchanged significance depending purely on the selection criterion, while only S. pyogenes remained significant under both. That the honest verdict for an endpoint depends on an essentially arbitrary methodological choice is direct evidence that, at this sample size, supervised selection is unstable and its apparent successes are not reproducible — reinforcing that only nested validation, and preferably an unsupervised or a priori descriptor choice, yields trustworthy conclusions."));
