// ================= RESULTS AND DISCUSSION =================
push(H1("Results and discussion"));
push(H2("Redundancy reduction is unsupervised and comes first"));
push(body("Large descriptor pools are highly redundant: constitutional, topological and connectivity indices frequently encode the same property. Collapsing each cluster of |ρ| or |r| > 0.7 descriptors to a single representative, while keeping uncorrelated singletons, removes redundancy without discarding chemically distinct information and is fully auditable. Crucially, this step uses no activity labels, so it is leakage-free and need not be nested — an important practical point, because it means aggressive redundancy reduction on the full dataset is statistically legitimate even in the small-n regime."));
push(H2("Why both Spearman and Pearson"));
push(body("Pearson's r captures linear associations, Spearman's ρ any monotonic relationship. Using both (a pair is redundant if either exceeds 0.7) is the more conservative choice and, with count and binary descriptors at n = 31, catches monotonic-but-nonlinear redundancies that Pearson underestimates. In the interpretable set the two coefficients agreed on 25 of the redundant pairs; three were detected only by Spearman (most clearly hydrogen-bond-donor count vs. TPSA, r = 0.57 but ρ = 0.72) and two only by Pearson. The reduction differed by a single descriptor (13 vs. 14) — a representative swap within one redundant block — so the result is robust to the choice of coefficient."));
push(H2("Supervised filtering outside cross-validation invalidates apparent performance"));
push(body("The central result of this study is a cautionary one. Reducing a very large pool by correlation and then by a supervised effect-size filter, and reporting the cross-validated performance of the resulting model, appears to work — Route A gave positive apparent Q² and permutation p ≈ 0.0005 for four strains. This apparent success is, however, largely an artefact of selection bias. The effect-size filter uses the activity labels of all 31 compounds to choose descriptors; a left-out compound has therefore already influenced the selection, so the ordinary permutation test — which permutes labels only for the modelling step while holding the label-selected descriptors fixed — is circular and reports a spuriously small p. With p ≫ n some descriptors always separate a particular small dataset by chance, so this route effectively selects, and then rewards, stable noise [Ambroise & McLachlan; Varma & Simon; Rücker et al.]. When the entire selection is moved inside the cross-validation and the whole pipeline is permuted, the apparent ability disappears for H. influenzae and S. pyogenes (Q² < 0). In short: the widely used large-pool-plus-effect-size route, validated in the usual non-nested way, yields statistically invalid performance estimates. It must be checked by nested validation before any predictive claim is made."));
push(H2("The excess of univariate hits does not prove signal"));
push(body("A natural objection is that far more descriptors pass the effect-size filter than the ~110 (5 % of 2,208) expected by chance — 373 to 603 across the strains — so the surplus of several hundred descriptors ought to reflect genuine discrimination. That reasoning assumes 2,208 independent tests, which the data violate: the descriptors are strongly correlated, so the number passing by chance is not fixed near 110 but varies enormously. Permuting the class labels 1,000 times and recomputing the filter (Fig. 4) yields a null distribution with a mean of about 85–95 but a 95th percentile of ~285–300 and a maximum of 580–690, because a single fortuitous label split is echoed by whole families of correlated descriptors that pass together. The observed counts do exceed the null mean — consistent with some genuine signal, matching the strains that survive nested validation — yet they lie within the null maximum (for S. aureus the null reaches 692 against 603 observed). One therefore cannot subtract 110 and treat the remainder as real, nor identify which specific descriptors are the true positives; and because these p-values were not corrected for multiple testing, a surplus is expected even under weak signal. The honest counterpart of the excess-hits argument is the whole-pipeline permutation test, which asks whether the complete selection-and-modelling procedure predicts better than chance — and for most endpoints it does not."));
push(img("/mnt/user-data/outputs/Fig_correlation_inflation.png", 560, 137, "png"));
push(legend("4","Correlation inflates the number of descriptors passing the effect-size filter by chance. For each strain the null distribution (grey) is the count of descriptors with |r| > 0.3 and p ≤ 0.05 over 1,000 label permutations; the observed count (red) and the naïve independence expectation (5 % × 2,208 ≈ 110, dotted) are marked. Chance alone can produce up to ~580–690 hits — for S. aureus more than the 603 observed — so the surplus over 110 is not evidence that hundreds of descriptors genuinely discriminate."));
push(body("The same caution applies to visual evidence. Two-dimensional PLS-DA score plots of the selected descriptors show convincing separation of active and inactive compounds for every strain (Additional file, Figs S6–S7), including the strains that fail nested validation. Apparent separation on the training data, whether summarised as a high apparent Q², a small ordinary permutation p, or a clean score plot, is a property of fitting a flexible model to few samples in many dimensions — not evidence of predictive ability."));
push(H2("Yet nested validation can still succeed — the procedure is not worthless"));
push(body("Nesting does not simply destroy all signal. Despite only 31 compounds, several of them barely active, the fully nested analysis remained significant for S. aureus and S. pneumoniae (Q² 0.16 and 0.19; p ≤ 0.013). The supervised route can therefore be meaningful for some datasets — provided the performance is estimated by nested validation rather than by the biased non-nested procedure. The lesson is not to abandon supervised selection, but to validate it honestly: filtering is permitted, but the performance estimate must be nested."));
push(H2("A small, interpretable, unsupervised-reduced set is the statistically cleaner route"));
push(body("Route B avoids the problem at its source. Because the descriptors are fixed a priori from mechanistic knowledge and the only data-driven step (correlation grouping) is unsupervised, there is no label leakage to correct, and a simple permutation test is already honest. This route recovered a genuine, moderate and interpretable signal for three strains with a single latent variable, and its apparent and cross-validated performance agree. A pre-selected set of this kind therefore carries real, if modest, predictive value, and its descriptors can be interpreted mechanistically without the circularity that afflicts data-driven selection. The trade-off is that a priori selection is subjective; but subjectivity that is transparent and label-independent is preferable, in the small-n regime, to an objective-looking but leaky data-driven filter."));
push(H2("Disadvantages of two-stage VIP-based selection"));
push(body("Selecting descriptors by VIP in a first model and re-using only the high-VIP ones in a second is attractive but has two drawbacks. First, VIP is a model-internal quantity: the mean squared VIP is 1 by construction, so some descriptors exceed 1 in every model, including one fitted to noise; VIP > 1 is a ranking heuristic within an already-validated model, not independent evidence of predictive value. Second, and more seriously, VIP is computed from the labels, so selecting on it outside cross-validation is the same leakage as any supervised filter — our naive-versus-nested comparison shows the resulting Q² is inflated (e.g. 0.29 vs. 0.16). We therefore recommend using VIP only for interpretation within a validated model; if it is used for selection, that selection must be nested. With a well-chosen a priori set the second VIP stage is usually unnecessary, because the reduced set already models honestly."));
push(H2("Why the n:p ratio governs the whole design"));
push(body("All of the above is a consequence of the n:p ratio. At n = 31 the data support only a few effective dimensions; feeding a large pool into PLS-DA guarantees apparent separation, and even a modest supervised filter can manufacture it. Reducing p — unsupervised where possible — and reporting one- or two-component models keeps the ratio in the range where the cross-validated estimate is meaningful. The rule p ≤ n/2 is a useful ceiling, but it is not sufficient on its own: a 15-descriptor model with a supervised, non-nested selection is still biased. The n:p constraint and honest (nested) validation are complementary, not interchangeable. The exchange of significant strains between the two selection criteria (Table 1C, Fig. 2c) is a further symptom of this instability: when the honest verdict for an endpoint flips with an arbitrary methodological choice, the apparent signal is not reproducible."));
push(H2("Mechanistic interpretability of the retained descriptors"));
push(body("The descriptors retained by Route B are consistent with the known determinants of monoterpenoid antibacterial activity — hydrogen-bonding capacity, molecular size/surface and lipophilicity, together with functional-group indicators (phenol, carbonyl, alcohol) — in line with the reported membrane-directed mode of action [Griffin et al.; refs]. That a leakage-free, label-independent reduction retains mechanistically plausible descriptors supports the interpretation that the Route-B models reflect a real, if moderate, structure–activity relationship rather than a numerical artefact."));
push(H2("Effect of class balance"));
push(body("Because activity was defined by MIC, the active/non-active split is not balanced (from 3/28 to 11/20). At this scale, moderate imbalance affects power only marginally; the dominant constraint is n:p. A more subtle point is that the supervised effect-size filter is unstable under strong imbalance: with very few actives, more descriptors pass the filter by chance (P. aeruginosa retained more features than the better-balanced strains), which is a symptom of instability rather than of stronger discrimination and reinforces the need for nested validation."));

// ================= CONCLUSIONS =================
push(H1("Conclusions"));
push(body("Using 31 monoterpenoids against five bacterial strains, we compared the two descriptor-selection routes that dominate small-sample QSAR and examined how they should be validated. Our central finding is methodological: the popular route of computing a very large descriptor pool, reducing it by correlation and a supervised effect-size (or VIP) filter, and reporting the cross-validated performance of the resulting model, produces optimistically biased and effectively invalid performance estimates, because the label-based selection is performed outside cross-validation and the usual permutation test is then circular. Only fully nested validation, with the selection repeated inside every fold and the whole pipeline permuted, gives an honest estimate — under which the apparent ability collapsed for two of the four ostensibly significant strains. Nevertheless, and despite very few and weakly active compounds, a genuine signal survived nesting for two strains, so the supervised route can be worthwhile for some datasets when validated honestly. The complementary route — a small, mechanistically chosen, interpretable descriptor set reduced only by unsupervised correlation — is leakage-free by construction, recovered a genuine moderate signal for three strains, and is our recommended default for the small-n regime."));
push(new Paragraph({ spacing:{before:60,after:60}, children:[new TextRun({text:"Recommendations for descriptor selection under an unfavourable n:p ratio.", bold:true, font:FONT, size:22})]}));
push(body("Synthesising these results, we suggest the following for datasets where p approaches or exceeds n: (1) Prefer a small, mechanistically motivated, interpretable descriptor set over an exhaustive data-driven pool; theory-based pre-selection is subjective but label-independent and therefore leakage-free. (2) Reduce residual redundancy by unsupervised correlation grouping (Spearman or Pearson > 0.7); this may be done on the full dataset without bias. (3) Keep any supervised selection (effect-size, VIP, wrappers) strictly inside the cross-validation; never select on labels using all data and then report the cross-validated score of the reduced model. (4) Report the nested cross-validated Q²/AUC as the performance estimate and the apparent values only as goodness-of-fit; validate significance by permuting the entire pipeline, not just the final model. (5) Respect the n:p ceiling (p ≤ n/2 as a guide) and prefer one- to two-component models. (6) Correct for multiplicity across endpoints, and treat all results as hypothesis-generating until confirmed on an external test set. Descriptors selected in this way should be described as associated with activity in the dataset, not as validated predictors of new compounds. Following these steps, honest and interpretable structure–activity signals can be extracted even from small, imperfect datasets, while the spurious models that an unfavourable n:p ratio would otherwise produce are avoided."));

